ivisible by \\(9\\).\n\n### Step 3: Sum of Distinct Possible Values of \\(GH\\)\nGiven \\(H = 5\\), we need \\(G + 5\\) to be divisible by \\(9\\):\n- \\(G = 4\\) (since \\(4 + 5 = 9\\) and \\(4 + 5 = 9\\))\n- \\(G = 3\\) (since \\(3 + 5 = 8\\) and \\(4 + 5 = 9\\))\n- \\(G = 2\\) (since \\(2 + 5 = 7\\) and \\(4 + 5 = 9\\))\n- \\(\\ldots\\)\n- \\(G = 0\\) (since \\(0 + 5 = 5\\) and \\(4 + 5 = 9\\))\n\nLet’s sum all the distinct values of \\(G\\) such that \\(G + 5\\) is divisible by \\(9\\):\n\\[4\\ +\\ 3,\\ +\\ 2,\\ +\\ 1,\\ +\\ 0 = 10\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all distinct possible values of the product \\(GH\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
